Optimal Solution - Implements string-based multiplication using the classic digit-by-digit multiplication algorithm.
                   Multiply digits from right to left, store results in an array of size m + n, and handle carries on the fly.

class Solution {
    public String multiply(String num1, String num2) {
        // Lengths of the two input numbers
        int m = num1.length();
        int n = num2.length();
        // Result array can have at most m + n digits
        int[] result = new int[m + n];
        // Multiply each digit of num1 with each digit of num2 (grade-school method)
        for (int i = m - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            for (int j = n - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
                // Multiply the current digits
                int product = (num1.charAt(i) - '0') * (num2.charAt(j) - '0');
                // Add product to the existing value at the corresponding position
                int sum = product + result[i + j + 1];
                // Store the unit digit at position i + j + 1
                result[i + j + 1] = sum % 10;
                // Carry the tens digit to position i + j
                result[i + j] += sum / 10;
            }
        }
        // Build the final result string from the array
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int digit : result) {
            // Skip leading zeros
            if (!(sb.length() == 0 && digit == 0)) {
                sb.append(digit);
            }
        }
        // If all digits were zero, return "0"
        return sb.length() == 0 ? "0" : sb.toString();
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(m * n).
Space Complexity :- O(m + n).
